and face sunscreens on your body the only&nbsp;
minor issue is that body products tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:09.760
to spread more easily and be a bit heavier&nbsp;
but if you're fine with that then it's fine

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:14.960
uv protective clothing is fantastic but&nbsp;
obviously some skin is usually uncovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:19.520
regular clothes can work as well depending on&nbsp;
the fabric hats and umbrellas are useful but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:24.480
most of the uv you get actually gets bounced from&nbsp;
the sky rather than coming directly from the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.040
so the lower parts of your face actually get a&nbsp;
lot less protection so these are good to use in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.240
conjunction with sunscreen to add extra layers of&nbsp;
protection think of it like layers of swiss cheese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:39.040
layering enough types of imperfect protection&nbsp;
will give you pretty good protection overall

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:42.880
can i mix sunscreen with foundation&nbsp;
or moisturizer or another sunscreen?

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:47.840
it's best not to mix sunscreens with any other&nbsp;
products even though it can make it feel nicer or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:52.640
get rid of white casts or make your routine faster&nbsp;
it changes the way the sunscreen dries which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:57.200
mean you get holes in your protection sunscreens&nbsp;
are emulsions like mayonnaise if you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:01.840
wrong proportions then the emulsion can break&nbsp;
and separate if you've ever seen how foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:06.240
can bunch up if you apply it over the wrong&nbsp;
product the same thing can happen to sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:11.120
and if that's happening on a microscopic level&nbsp;
you won't be able to spot it and stop doing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:15.120
it's best to just look for a sunscreen that&nbsp;
already does what you want if you don't want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:19.920
white cast then go for a chemical sunscreen if&nbsp;
you want a tint then go for tinted sunscreen

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.440
even worse are those sunscreen drop&nbsp;
products that you're meant to mix into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.760
a moisturizer to make your own sunscreen&nbsp;
this means that you have this holey film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:32.960
issue plus you end up applying so little&nbsp;
of it that you end up with really low spf

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:35.520
can i make my own diy sunscreen?

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:39.840
so i mentioned the sunscreen drop products that&nbsp;
you mix into other products end up with really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:45.360
low protection and unreliable film formation&nbsp;
there are also diy sunscreen recipes that involve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:51.760
mixing zinc oxide powder or diaper cream into oils&nbsp;
or moisturizers to get around 20% zinc oxide which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:57.120
matches the percentage in a store-bought sunscreen&nbsp;
this doesn't work because protection isn't about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:01.920
the percentage of the active ingredients&nbsp;
it's also about how they're distributed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:07.520
the sunscreen and how that applies on the skin&nbsp;
sunscreen uses special grades of zinc oxide with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:14.320
very small particles this makes it absorb more uv&nbsp;
and it can also cover a larger area even with 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:18.960
of this special zinc oxide mixed with industrial&nbsp;
equipment that can make the zinc oxide spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:23.920
out more evenly in the sunscreen sunscreen&nbsp;
formulators still get low spf sometimes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:29.200
is because the base isn't keeping that zinc oxide&nbsp;
spread out properly when you apply it to your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:34.720
so sunscreen formulators can't get consistently&nbsp;
high spf with all of this special stuff the chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:40.240
of you getting it right consistently at home&nbsp;
with the wrong kind of zinc oxide is not great

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.200
can i use expired sunscreen?

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:47.520
there's no guarantee the sunscreen will be&nbsp;
working after the expiry date so i would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.400
recommend throwing expired sunscreen out also&nbsp;
don't store sunscreen in the heat it can break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:57.440
down and separate even before it expires which&nbsp;
means you won't get even protection so don't store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:02.400
it in your car glove box and if you're at the&nbsp;
beach or at the park try to keep it in the shade

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.640
do i need to reapply sunscreen?

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:10.320
the main reason you need to reapply sunscreen is&nbsp;
that the sunscreen layer gets less even over time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:14.560
like foundation the sunscreen layer clumps&nbsp;
up over time because your skin is moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.960
and producing sweat and oil underneath that&nbsp;
means you'll start getting tiny gaps in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:24.240
sunscreen layer that the uv can get through some&nbsp;
of the sunscreen also evaporates into the air and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:29.280
absorbs into your skin so that sunscreen layer&nbsp;
will get thinner and less effective sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:33.920
can also get wiped off onto things and if you&nbsp;
wet the sunscreen layer that can also clump up

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:38.960
even if it's a water resistant sunscreen so&nbsp;
after you've had your sunscreen on for two hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:43.600
you're probably not getting that label protection&nbsp;
anymore which is an issue if you're about to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:48.240
more sun exposure important note this is&nbsp;
two hours of the sunscreen being on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.880
skin not two hours of sun exposure there's&nbsp;
this misconception that reapplying sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:57.840
is because the sunscreen ingredients stop&nbsp;
working after absorbing two hours of sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:02.480
this is a myth most sunscreens are pretty&nbsp;
photostable now so the ingredients almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:06.960
always keep working for longer than&nbsp;
the sunscreen film takes to clump up

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:11.200
this two hours is also just a general rule&nbsp;
there's evidence that some sunscreens can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:16.160
last a lot longer and obviously if you're&nbsp;
sweating or moving around a lot and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:21.600
sunscreen isn't water resistant it probably&nbsp;
won't be staying put if you aren't moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:26.000
around a lot then you probably still have&nbsp;
pretty decent protection after a few hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:31.440
but no one's tested the protection for a&nbsp;
wide range of sunscreens so it's really hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:36.800
to say how much and unfortunately you can't&nbsp;
really rely on what you see with a uv camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:41.760
uv cameras don't have enough resolution to&nbsp;
tell what's going on and they work on uva&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:48.080
only one study found that the uv photo looked&nbsp;
exactly the same even with around 15% less spf

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.000
how do i reapply sunscreen?

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:54.880
i think reapplying sunscreen on your body&nbsp;
is pretty straightforward but how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:59.680
reapply it on your face and how do you do&nbsp;
it if you're wearing makeup unfortunately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.680 --> 00:19:04.320
there isn't really a perfect way to get high&nbsp;
protection while keeping your makeup intact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:08.880
so i approach it depending on how much&nbsp;
sun exposure i'm going to get afterwards

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:13.440
situation one is if i'm getting a lot of&nbsp;
sun exposure say i'm going on a long beach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:18.080
walk in the afternoon then i keep my makeup&nbsp;
simple and just reapply the full amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:22.080
sunscreen like normal every two hours&nbsp;
followed by minimal makeup you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:26.240
have to wash off your sunscreen you can&nbsp;
just reapply on top but if there's dirt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:30.880
on your face or if it's just getting a bit&nbsp;
messy you can wipe it off before you reapply

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:36.720
situation two is if i'm getting some sun and i&nbsp;
want to keep my makeup mostly intact say it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:41.600
summer and i'm walking outside for 20 minutes&nbsp;
after work then i reapply sunscreen on top of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:46.720
makeup right before i go out there are only really&nbsp;
two options for this that i think give enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:52.160
protection the option i prefer is reapplying&nbsp;
a light regular sunscreen over my makeup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:57.840
i use a cushion puff which is a method that jude&nbsp;
of 50 shades of snail came up with around 2015&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:01.520
i really love these because they're so thin&nbsp;
they're really portable and they don't absorb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:06.480
much sunscreen it is still good to use a bit more&nbsp;
sunscreen than normal to make up for what ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.480 --> 00:20:10.720
in the sponge there are sunscreen cushions&nbsp;
from asian brands as well that are designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:16.400
for reapplying over makeup but i find those too&nbsp;
expensive plus they usually have a white cast

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:21.760
the other option that can give decent coverage is&nbsp;
a spray sprays can get into the valleys of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:28.000
skin but the main issue is that sprays can be a&nbsp;
bit light in some spots so you need to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:33.680
you apply enough layers so if you're using a spray&nbsp;
make sure you apply enough layers let each layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:38.960
dry down before you apply the next layer you can&nbsp;
spray your spray into a quarter teaspoon measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:44.000
to work out how many sprays you need the other&nbsp;
issue is that breathing in too much sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:48.800
probably isn't very good for you so if you use&nbsp;
a spray make sure you hold your breath and walk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:53.120
out of the cloud of spray before you breathe&nbsp;
in again also be careful if it's too windy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:58.880
because the spray just won't land on your skin i&nbsp;
would also avoid aerosol sunscreens the spf test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:03.520
is usually done before adding propellant so you&nbsp;
need to apply a lot more to get that labelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:08.240
protection for re-application it's always safest&nbsp;
to go with a sunscreen that has similar active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:12.400
ingredients to what you're already wearing&nbsp;
there's a slight chance that the sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:16.960
actives can react with each other and break&nbsp;
down if you aren't mixing them around a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:21.360
then it shouldn't be a huge problem but&nbsp;
it is safest to try to avoid that anyway

00:21:21.360 --> 00:21:26.640
situation 3 is when i get minimal sun so for&nbsp;
example i'm going outside for 5 minutes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:31.360
it's mostly shady in that case i don't&nbsp;
reapply i rely on what i have on already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:36.480
and just try to stay in the shade you can&nbsp;
also use those layers of protection so hats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:41.760
shades sunglasses umbrellas are great if you&nbsp;
don't think your reapplication was quite enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:45.040
and also just generally when&nbsp;
you're getting a lot of sun

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:48.160
can i transfer sunscreen&nbsp;
into a different container?

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:52.480
i would really recommend not doing&nbsp;
that especially not for a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:58.000
a lot of sunscreen ingredients can react with or&nbsp;
soak into some plastics this means you can end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:02.880
with less active ingredients in the sunscreen and&nbsp;
the sunscreen can dry in weird ways on your skin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:06.960
glass should be okay if it's very clean&nbsp;
and if you keep it protected from light

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:09.520
how do i deal with eye sting?

00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:12.640
some sunscreens can sting your eyes and annoyingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:16.320
it seems to be different ingredients&nbsp;
and sunscreens for different people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:21.280
there are some sunscreens out there that don't&nbsp;
sting your eyes much so look at reviews for those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:25.840
but if you've found the perfect sunscreen&nbsp;
apart from eye sting you can use a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:30.560
sunscreen for around your eyes i like to use&nbsp;
a zinc oxide sunscreen stick because they tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:35.840
to stay put for longer and zinc oxide tends to&nbsp;
not make your eyes water some people also find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:41.120
that setting their sunscreen with translucent&nbsp;
powder helps but that isn't quite enough for me

00:22:41.120 --> 00:22:43.200
do i need to wear sunscreen indoors?

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:48.000
probably not you probably aren't&nbsp;
getting enough uv inside your house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:53.760
window glass blocks almost all the uvb but uva&nbsp;
can still come through and that can potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:59.440
cause premature aging and increased pigment but&nbsp;
the roof and the walls are probably blocking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:03.600
vast majority of it unless you have sunlight&nbsp;
directly hitting your skin through a window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:08.720
or if you're sitting next to a big window and you&nbsp;
can see lots of sky through it monitors and phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:13.360
screens don't produce enough damaging light to&nbsp;
worry about unless you have a special condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:18.400
and you would know if you have one of these&nbsp;
fluorescent lights also don't produce much uv if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:24.960
they're properly shielded maybe be careful if you&nbsp;
have a bare bulb particularly an older curly bulb

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:26.960
there are a lot of other myths about sunscreen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.960 --> 00:23:30.400
that i haven't covered here and i'm&nbsp;
sure there'll be even more to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:34.720
but here is a list of myths that i've debunked&nbsp;
before i've linked the long versions in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:40.960
description but here is my attempt to speedrun&nbsp;
the more common ones in 20 words or less each

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:45.920
"chemical sunscreens are dangerous hormonal&nbsp;
disruptors" legal limits based on all relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:51.760
data in context with large margin of safety&nbsp;
fear-mongering sites show select studies only

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:55.280
"nanoparticles are dangerous"&nbsp;
nanoparticles don't penetrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:57.680
beyond the stratum corneum with unbroken skin

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:02.320
"chemical and physical sunscreens work&nbsp;
differently" both mostly work the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:07.680
only five to ten percent uv is scattered&nbsp;
by physical sunscreens the rest is absorbed

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:11.680
"physical sunscreens work better"&nbsp;
how well sunscreen works depends on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:17.600
spf and uvapf chemical sunscreens&nbsp;
can get higher spfs and uvapfs

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:20.640
"heat from chemical sunscreens&nbsp;
is bad for melasma" chemical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:26.000
and physical sunscreens produce around&nbsp;
same heat that amount is undetectable

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:29.920
"some sunscreens aren't reef safe"&nbsp;
climate change agricultural pollution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:34.720
overfishing are killing reefs way too little&nbsp;
sunscreen in ocean to make a difference

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:38.080
"sunscreens contain carcinogenic&nbsp;
benzene" amounts in sunscreen are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:42.160
tiny you get more from parking your&nbsp;
car in a garage attached to your house

